#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124252 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ee40fd-e452-5593-9a35-7c46d1f0cca0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Constants in First-Order Partial Derivative Expressions"}] :question/id #uuid "2247dc40-0c71-596c-9da4-a3bffd85e106" :question/math-academy-id "e-3152" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b,$ if \n\n$$\nf_x(x,y) = 6xy+axy^3-5, \\qquad\nf_y(x,y) = 3x^2+bx^2y^2, \\qquad\nf_y(2,-1) = -24.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy+ax{y}^{3}-5) \\\\  & =6x+3ax{y}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =6x+3ax{y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+b{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =6x+2bx{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. So, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & ={f}_{yx}(x,y) \\\\ 6x+3ax{y}^{2} & =6x+2bx{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $xy^2,$ we see that $3a=2b.$ \n\nNow, since $f_y(2,-1)=-24,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,-1) & =-24 \\\\ 3(2)^{2}+b(2)^{2}(-1)^{2} & =-24 \\\\ 12+4b & =-24 \\\\ 4b & =-36 \\\\ b & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & =2b \\\\ 3a & =2(-9) \\\\ 3a & =-18 \\\\ a & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-6$ and $b=-9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124253 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7619454-7a2b-5c0b-927c-24dca2e564c3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions of Partial Derivatives at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "d45926a5-0126-50b4-bdc7-a9f686408986" :question/math-academy-id "e-3153" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y,z).$ Given that all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial z\\partial x} = -e^xy\\sin{(yz)},\n$$\n\n \n\nevaluate the following expression at the point $(1,\\pi,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\partial z\\partial x\\partial z}+ \\dfrac{\\partial^3 f }{\\partial x \\partial z \\partial y}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂z∂x} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂z∂x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-{e}^{x}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂z∂x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{x}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)-{e}^{x}yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)+yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,\\pi,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)} & =-{e}^{(1)}(\\pi )^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)=e{\\pi }^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)} & =-{e}^{(1)}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)+(\\pi )(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1))=e\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)} & =e{\\pi }^{2}+e\\pi =e\\pi (\\pi +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124254 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/id #uuid "d62e8502-b3c5-5923-8ab3-ef988507cd2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3154" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- $f_x(x,y)=x+2y$ and $f_y(x,y)=2y-x$\n\n- $f_x(x,y)=5(x+y)^4$ and $f_y(x,y)=5(x+y)^4$\n\n- $f_x(x,y)=-\\sin{x}\\sin{y}$ and $f_y(x,y)=\\cos{x}\\cos{y}$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is,\n\n$$\nf_{xy}(x,y)=f_{yx}(x,y).\n$$\n\nLet's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+2y)=2, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y) \\neq f_{yx}(x,y),$ the first pair could *not* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5(x+y)^{4})=20(x+y)^{3}, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5(x+y)^{4})=20(x+y)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y) = f_{yx}(x,y),$ the second pair *could* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y)=f_{yx}(x,y),$  the third pair *could* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a8df045-f6bd-568e-87e8-8e1f2a4ff801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266367} {:answer/id #uuid "31ca1f55-b38d-57f6-b27b-6af7eb864f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266583} {:answer/id #uuid "84209361-266f-56a2-90fa-973bc7afcdb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267307} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad26291-e462-58d0-a659-c844150d9ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267385} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a59cf5-a6cf-570e-a6fd-760929f3b02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5a59cf5-a6cf-570e-a6fd-760929f3b02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267767} :answer-field/id #uuid "a974d11a-8dca-5ec8-b4db-241f08aa75bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53d0afb1-1358-5254-8618-617a2b1cbc7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-133250" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=8xy-5{x}^{3}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=6x{y}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2})=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy)=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y)=8y-15{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy-5{x}^{3})=8y-15{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(9{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=18{x}^{2}y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{3})=6{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "884d0021-241f-59ee-ac20-35d8dc83812d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383344} {:answer/id #uuid "307a8a24-1c7b-5a9b-9984-fe97db53d476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383345} {:answer/id #uuid "53614062-42da-5993-a88a-ef98686557fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383346} {:answer/id #uuid "73c81600-9ccb-5055-90c7-5c358c405cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383347} {:answer/id #uuid "aa18bb00-52dd-5f02-8e04-cdb69157970d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "307a8a24-1c7b-5a9b-9984-fe97db53d476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383345} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3b17f5c-b92c-5712-ad37-ec88c97f8a52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d4e7c93-f352-516f-9253-b49a36a438e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-133297" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2{e}^{2x-{y}^{2}}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-2y{e}^{2x-{y}^{2}}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-{y}^{2})$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-{y}^{2})$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{e}^{2x-{y}^{2}})=-4y{e}^{2x-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y{e}^{2x+{y}^{2}})=-4y{e}^{2x-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-{y}^{2}))=4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-{y}^{2}) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-{y}^{2}))=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-{y}^{2})+4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x})=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7619454-7a2b-5c0b-927c-24dca2e564c3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions of Partial Derivatives at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75579bca-c34c-5a92-92cc-5b55f8bdaaac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267177} {:answer/id #uuid "7c90f5b0-0888-50f1-8484-0af0b95e4816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267245} {:answer/id #uuid "88809b54-c29c-5c05-b0df-ca0ca0df409b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267353} {:answer/id #uuid "b44cf9cd-8721-5e6e-82a2-6913bbcd5ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267754} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ec9fd6-bba7-53b7-a39d-c6c8cd502cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88809b54-c29c-5c05-b0df-ca0ca0df409b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267353} :answer-field/id #uuid "98686903-fbc8-549f-8e19-42cefa5217e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4cca6146-d9c7-5b1f-ad16-9d00e8365a81" :question/math-academy-id "q-133395" :question/problem "Evaluate ${f}_{yzx}(2,1,3)$ given that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n{f}_{xy}(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x-y+z}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yzx}(x,y,z) & ={f}_{xyz}(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({f}_{xz}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{x-y+z}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-y+z}}-\\frac{1}{x+y+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point  $(2,1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yzx}(2,1,3) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2-1+3}}-\\frac{1}{2+1+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
